Strengths:
Informative in-loop diagnostics:
  - corner pixel error binned by gate size shows that precision, not recall, was limiting;
  - duplicate detections are shown to collapse success to 15.6%;
  - EKF next-gate error binned by distance localizes where the methods differ.

Weaknesses:
The central motivation, avoiding a second network in the onboard budget, is not validated:
  - module and post-processing latency, memory and size on the Orin are not measured;
  - neither is GateNet's cost;
  - the core alone already consumes 26.4 of the 33.3 ms frame budget;
  - the NearGateHead is itself a dedicated detector.

Missing controls and ablations:
  - no GateNet with the same global NMS and min-size filter, which is essential to attribute gains to the frozen-core modules rather than post-processing;
  - no leave-one-module-out ablation of the final configuration; the CornerRefiner showed no measurable gain alone yet is retained;
  - no adapter/LoRA or fine-tuned-copy baselines with reported general-metric deltas;
  - no compute comparison against running a separate specialized network.

Minor issues:
  - the core input is described as 448 px, while timings and deployment use 384×384;
  - a note on TensorRT/fp16 determinism is needed for the bit-identity claim;
  - some references are inaccurate;
  - the far-gate (8+ m) regression is partly caused by the min-size filter.

Limitations:
The authors acknowledge the key limitations: simulation-only closed-loop results, one application and one baseline, a non-significant advantage, worse far-gate estimator error, unmeasured module latency, and the unmatched min-size sweep. Further limitations:
  - the frozen-core design caps achievable precision at what the frozen features encode;
  - total compute and memory grow with each added module;
  - there is no comparison against a separate specialized network;
  - module details are insufficient for reproduction;
  - modules trained on simulator data raise sim-to-real concerns;
  - the 'offline metrics mislead' lesson is untested beyond EKF duplicate sensitivity.
Societal impact is minimal, though autonomous high-speed drone perception has dual-use potential that could be briefly noted.
